  "about": "The app-path grammar's cases, read by both `src/volume/path_grammar_test.rs` and `apps/desktop/src/lib/path/grammar.test.ts`, so Rust and TypeScript can't disagree. `flavor` is the local backend's: `posix` (macOS, Linux) or `windows`. `kind` null means the text is no app path at all. A scheme URL's root, root-ness, and segments belong to its volume, not the grammar, so they read null; its parent and basename are plain slash arithmetic. `parent` is null at a root: there is no parent inside the volume. `shown` is what a person reads; `fromUserInput` is what typed or pasted text becomes before it goes on the wire. `arithmetic` rows (read by the Rust suite) pin path arithmetic per root shape: `std` is what `std::path` answers today on macOS and Linux (`names` is `components()`, root included; `rebase` is `app_paths::rebase`; `shown` is `Path::display`), and `target` is the typed app path's answer with the base parsed in `flavor` (`names` without the root; a scheme root is `scheme://authority`), or `refused` names the typed refusal. A `note` marks a row where the two differ on purpose.",
